+# Editorial review — Movement 6, chapters 38–44
+
+## Review status and coverage
+
+This is a fresh-context, informed editorial review by a model that did not draft the movement. It is not a blind cold read or a real human audience test. I read all seven supplied chapters in order, chapters 38–44, together with the compiled canon evidence, formula instructions and movement brief. Coverage is 37,523 prose words. The movement is complete in chapter count, but not release-ready because three coordinator placeholders remain in the manuscript.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest caught hard when Oryn named the violation in her own terms: “You turned my table round” (chapter 38, lines 108–134). That scene gives her full agency, refuses easy absolution and makes Cael's progression morally consequential. The strongest person in the movement is Oryn; Lira is close behind, particularly in the one-handed purse work, shooting lessons and the quiet “Then six.” Karis's refusal to turn inference into fact gives the mystery unusual credibility, and Brom's forge story supplies warmth without softening the consequences.
+
+Interest slipped somewhat during the consecutive method explanations from the “one basket” discussion through the naming, looms, strata and stopped hypothesis (chapters 41–43). Each scene earns its place, but the same principle—observe, do not overclaim—is sometimes explained again after the concrete image has already made it clear. The three visible `[[TIER-B]]` markers are a more serious interruption: each breaks an emotionally or logically important exchange.
+
+I expect the next movement to turn the four “wheres” into a dangerous second-site expedition, force Cael's new rule to survive pressure, and bring Oryn back into contact with the group on her own terms. I would continue. The movement pays off the tenth fragment, Cael's nineteenth birthday and the naming of the Quieting while opening a larger geographical and institutional problem.
+
+## Reader lenses
+
+Scores are editorial judgments, not measurements or release gates. Confidence reflects this seven-chapter read, not demographic research.
+
+| Dimension | Fluent thirteen-year-old | Adult genre reader | Exact passage and reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---:|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 7 | 8 | Chapter 38, lines 1–31: the brass plate and eleven silent seconds create immediate personal mystery, though the opening assumes substantial prior knowledge and begins with explanation rather than present action. | High |
+| Keep reading | 8 | 9 | Chapter 40, lines 325–383, and chapter 44, lines 187–217: the self-reading finds an unmoving place, then Oryn requires three readings and the log commits the group to finding the other circles. Local payoff and forward pressure are both strong. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 8 | 9 | Chapter 41, lines 157–217, and chapter 42, lines 191–279: naming a process by grammar and dating an impossible floor through tool marks and weathering are specific, fresh problems rather than generic lore delivery. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 6 | 7 | Chapters 41–43 repeatedly restate the distinction between evidence and hypothesis, and the placeholders at 38:181, 39:275 and 44:105 halt one-pass reading altogether. The concrete demonstrations remain clear. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9 | 9 | Chapter 38, lines 108–179; chapter 40, lines 3–41; chapter 43, lines 319–347: Oryn sets the price, Lira prepares the birthday without excusing Cael, and the gate-walk disclosure lands through restraint rather than sentimentality. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 9 | 8 | Chapter 40, lines 57–107 and 261–315: the three kinds of apple, Seln's administrative birthday form and Karis finishing the keeper apple give the group pleasure rooted in distinct personalities. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 7 | 7 | Chapter 39, lines 135–247, and chapter 42, lines 113–189 and 281–357: the Wind-economy drill and Lira's braced shooting have trackable problems, attempts and corrections. Suspense is intentionally modest in this quiet learning movement. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9 | 9 | Chapter 39, lines 57–247, and chapter 40, lines 317–383: the tenth fragment changes perception, exposes the hidden cost of Wind and enables a self-reading with a firm limit. Capability, cost and consequence are all visible. | High |
+| Connection | 9 | 9 | Chapter 42, lines 93–111 and 349–357; chapter 43, lines 113–213; chapter 44, lines 1–139: Lira's sling, ankle and tibia, Brom's hand and bow hook, Oryn's route arithmetic, the mule, the Fallow report and Vastin window all carry state forward cleanly. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 6 | 8 | The character voices and recurring concrete cadences read well, especially Brom's forge story (chapter 39, lines 93–125). The three literal markers are unacceptable in audio, while several long explanatory paragraphs in chapters 41–43 need careful breath phrasing. | High |
+
+### Reader Standard
+
+The movement passes the thirteen-year-old Reader Standard in content and moral framing. There is no profanity, obscenity, sexual content or innuendo. Violence is recalled through injury and consequence rather than gore. Most importantly, Cael's act against Oryn's consent is named as wrong by Oryn, Cael and the companions; confession does not purchase forgiveness, and Oryn controls the conditions and timing of any future reading. Courage, loyalty, restraint, honest uncertainty and care for injured people all carry real costs.
+
+The conceptual material is denser than the readability score suggests, but it is usually grounded in an apple, a loom, a stone, a hand or a bow. A fluent thirteen-year-old should be able to follow it. The chief comprehension risks are the missing lines and the repeated fine distinctions among observation, finding, hypothesis and fear.
+
+## Continuity, canon and protected wording
+
+### Continuity and power limits
+
+The movement's surviving state is coherent with the supplied ledger and packet:
+
+- The chronology runs from minute twenty-one on day 100 through Oryn's road window on day 112. Cael turns nineteen on day 102 without attaching the birthday to a season or interval.
+- Cael has ten confirmed fragments. The Tide-adjacent fragment is used only on himself; it listens rather than pulls, remains contact/self-limited, and does nothing in combat. The place found in the first self-reading is located but not named “the still place.”
+- Deployment since the Line remains thirteen. The eleven Wind leans are explicitly entered as contained yard practice outside the deployment number. Shadow is not used and Seln is not identified to Oryn as its source.
