+# Editorial review — Movement 6, chapters 38–44
+
+## Review status and coverage
+
+This is a fresh-context, informed editorial review by a model that did not draft the movement. It is not a blind cold read or a real human audience test. I read all seven supplied chapters in order, chapters 38–44, together with the compiled canon evidence, formula instructions and movement brief. Coverage is 37,523 prose words. The movement is complete in chapter count, but not release-ready because three coordinator placeholders remain in the manuscript.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest caught hard when Oryn named the violation in her own terms: “You turned my table round” (chapter 38, lines 108–134). That scene gives her full agency, refuses easy absolution and makes Cael's progression morally consequential. The strongest person in the movement is Oryn; Lira is close behind, particularly in the one-handed purse work, shooting lessons and the quiet “Then six.” Karis's refusal to turn inference into fact gives the mystery unusual credibility, and Brom's forge story supplies warmth without softening the consequences.
+
+Interest slipped somewhat during the consecutive method explanations from the “one basket” discussion through the naming, looms, strata and stopped hypothesis (chapters 41–43). Each scene earns its place, but the same principle—observe, do not overclaim—is sometimes explained again after the concrete image has already made it clear. The three visible `[[TIER-B]]` markers are a more serious interruption: each breaks an emotionally or logically important exchange.
+
+I expect the next movement to turn the four “wheres” into a dangerous second-site expedition, force Cael's new rule to survive pressure, and bring Oryn back into contact with the group on her own terms. I would continue. The movement pays off the tenth fragment, Cael's nineteenth birthday and the naming of the Quieting while opening a larger geographical and institutional problem.
